# Fableland — Foe System GDD

Design doc for the enemy/foe layer: types, stats, scaling, AI, skills, and evolution.
Covers everything that makes a fight work, from the moment a foe spawns to the moment it
dies (and what it leaves behind).

Implementation target: `Scripts/Foes/` (refactor from the current single `Scripts/Enemy.cs`).
First landed in **v0.4.0**.

---

## 1. Two-axis difficulty

A fight's difficulty is the product of two independent systems:

| Axis | Source | What it controls |
|------|--------|-----------------|
| **Map node level** (1–6) | Which node the player chose on the overworld | Objective complexity — wave count, foe count, win conditions, boss presence |
| **Foe level** (1–8) | How many days have passed (see §2) | Enemy stat scaling + skill access for every foe in every fight |

A player who grinds level-1 nodes still faces escalating foe levels as days pass.
A player who rushes a level-4 boss node on day 35 faces level-5 foes during a complex
objective. The two axes multiply: you can't out-grind the clock, and you can't out-rush
the stats.

---

## 2. Foe levels (day-based scaling)

Foe level is determined **purely by the current day**. It applies to every foe that
spawns, regardless of which map node the player is on.

| Foe Lv | Days | Difficulty | Skills unlocked | HP mult | ATK mult | SPD mult |
|--------|------|-----------|----------------|---------|----------|----------|
| 1 | 1–10 | **Fairytale** (童话般的) | — (patrol only) | ×1 | ×1 | ×1 |
| 2 | 11–20 | **Mundane** (平凡的) | Aggro (sight) | ×1.2 | ×1.2 | ×1.2 |
| 3 | 21–30 | **Mundane** (平凡的) | Sight improved | ×1.5 | ×1.5 | ×1.2 |
| 4 | 31–35 | **Challenging** (崎岖的) | Skill 1 | ×2 | ×1.5 | ×1.2 |
| 5 | 36–40 | **Challenging** (崎岖的) | Skill 1 | ×3 | ×1.5 | ×1.5 |
| 6 | 41–45 | **Demonic** (险恶的) | Skill 1 + Skill 2 | ×3 | ×2 | ×1.5 |
| 7 | zone 6 — LV5 nodes | **Hellish** (地狱般的) | Skill 1 + Skill 2 | ×4 | ×3 | ×1.5 |
| 8 | zone 6 — LV6 core | **God-Forbid** (神惧) | Skill 1 + Skill 2 | ×5 | ×3 | ×2 |

**Zone 6:** past the singularity the day is unknowable (`???`), so foe level is fixed
by the **node** instead of the day: the five `XX-5` fights spawn level-7 foes, the
`XX-6-1` core fight spawns level-8 foes. (The planned elapsed-time scaling of zone-6
defenders — `MapGDD.md` §8a anti-stagnation — remains TBD and would sit on top of this.)

**All multipliers are from base stats, not cumulative.** A level-8 Crab has 5× base HP,
3× base ATK, and 2× base SPD — calculated directly from the base values defined per foe
type (§3–4), not chained through previous levels.

"Skills unlocked" gates which abilities the foe has access to (see §3–4 per type).
Levels 2–3 add the sight/aggro system; levels 4+ unlock active skills one at a time.

---

## 3. Crab Foe

The grounded melee bruiser. A simplified port of the Unity BaseFoe/CrabFoe.

### Base stats

| Stat | Value |
|------|-------|
| Base HP | 70 |
| Base ATK | 18 |
| Base SPD | 90 px/s |
| Gravity | 2048 px/s² (Units.Gravity) |
| Contact range | 44 px |
| Contact cooldown | 0.9 s |
| Contact knockback | 260 px/s |
| Contact stun | 0.25 s |
| Hit radius | 26 px |
| External damping | 1200 px/s² |
| Burn DoT | 14/s |

### Passive — Soft Shell

Receives **200% damage** (2×) from any hit whose source is within a **60° cone above**
the crab (centered on the upward vertical). This rewards aerial attacks and plunging
strikes.

### Patrol

Moves horizontally in a random starting direction. On wall contact, turns around.

### Aggro (level 2+)

When the player is detected (see Sight), moves toward the player horizontally.

### Sight (level 2–3)

- **Shape:** 8×3 m rectangle, **ground-anchored** (extends upward from the crab's feet).
  Centered horizontally on the crab.
- **Check interval:** every 1 s.
- **De-aggro:** 3 consecutive misses → return to Patrol.

### Sight improved (level 3+)

- **Shape:** 12×5 m rectangle, same ground-anchored rule.

### Skill 1 — Spawn (level 4+, passive, on death)

On death, spawns **2 crabs** at `current level − 2`. The skill only exists at level 4+
(§2), so spawned crabs are always level 2 or higher (level 4 → two level-2 crabs,
level 5 → two level-3, … level 8 → two level-6). A level-3 crab that **evolves** to 4
mid-fight gains the skill. Spawned crabs **cannot themselves spawn** (a foe created by
Spawn ignores this skill — no grandchildren).

Spawned crabs appear near the parent's death position (small random offset).

### Skill 2 — Jump (level 6+, 5 s cooldown)

Triggers when **aggro'd** and **either**:
1. The player jumps, **or**
2. The player is within 3 m (96 px).

The crab jumps to the **same height as the player's current jump**. Does **not** deal
landing damage (pure repositioning / anti-kite tool).

---

## 4. Seagull Foe

The aerial harasser. No gravity, momentum-based movement, zoning tools.

### Base stats

| Stat | Value |
|------|-------|
| Base HP | 35 |
| Base ATK | 20 — the code's since v0.5.0 (the first design was 18) |
| Base SPD | (accel-based, see Passive) |
| Gravity | **none** (ignores gravity) |
| Contact damage | yes, at its ATK, every 0.9 s — on since v0.6.5 (the first design had none) — but **not while it dashes**, so the dash's own hit lands (v0.12.4; TBD #39) |
| Hit radius | 20 px |
| External damping | 600 px/s² |

### Passive — Flight

- Does **not** receive gravity.
- Direction-changing **acceleration = 1/3 of the player's** (`PlayerGroundAccel / 3`).
  This gives the seagull momentum — it can't instantly reverse, creating a window for
  the player.
- Turn rate applies horizontally only; vertical position is maintained during patrol
  and adjusted only by skills.

### Patrol

Flies at a fixed height until it reaches **100 m** (3200 px) from its spawn point, then
turns around and flies back. Height is set at spawn (configurable, default ~3–5 m above
ground).

### Aggro (level 2+)

When the player is detected (see Sight), accelerates horizontally toward the player's
X position. Vertical position is maintained (the seagull doesn't dive during basic aggro).

### Sight (level 2–3)

- **Shape:** 60° cone, 10 m length, facing the **direction of movement**.
- **When stationary:** retains last facing direction.
- **Check interval:** every 2 s.
- **De-aggro:** 3 consecutive misses → return to Patrol.

### Sight improved (level 3+)

- **Shape:** 120° cone, 12 m length.

### Skill 1 — Poop (from level 1 since v0.5.3, 5 s cooldown)

*v0.5.3 made it the seagull's basic attack, available at every level: gated at level 4 with
no contact damage, a low-level seagull was harmless.*

When **aggro'd**: drops a poop projectile that falls with gravity. On reaching a surface,
it **lingers for 3 s**, dealing **30 base damage** on contact — **once to each body** (v0.12.4:
before, the puddle hit four times a second, up to 360). The lingering poop is a
hazard — it zones the player and can stack with multiple seagulls.

Damage: 30 base (× ATK multiplier from foe level). Does the poop deal damage mid-air on
direct hit? **Yes** — direct hit during the drop also deals 30 base damage.

### Skill 2 — Dash (level 6+, 10 s cooldown)

When **aggro'd**: the seagull **stops moving for 1 s** and **turns red** (telegraph),
then **dashes 15 m** (480 px) **at 30 m/s** (0.5 s) toward the player's position at the
start of the telegraph — **aimed in both axes** in the code since v0.5.0 (the first design said
horizontal; at its patrol height of 3–5 m a level dash would pass over a grounded player). **Pierces through** the player (does not stop on contact). Deals
**50 base damage** on contact during the dash.

Damage: 50 base (× ATK multiplier from foe level).

---

## 5. Evolution (in-combat)

A foe that is **not eliminated within 25 s** of spawning **evolves** to the next level.

### Rules

- **+1 foe level** (e.g., level 3 → level 4).
- A foe can evolve **at most once**.
- Level 8 foes **cannot evolve** (already at cap).
- **HP:** max HP re-derives from base (`newMax = baseHP × mult(new)`); current HP is
  ratio-preserved, then the foe heals 30% of the new max:
  `hp = min(newMax, hp × mult(new)/mult(old) + 0.30 × newMax)`.
- **No interruption** — evolution happens instantly mid-action. The foe does not pause,
  channel, or reset its current behaviour.
- **Visual tell** (deferred): the foe grows larger, changes color, or swaps sprite.
  For now a brief flash or tint change is sufficient.

### Design intent

Evolution punishes passive play — if the player kites indefinitely without committing
to a kill, the foe escalates. The 30% heal means the player keeps most of their progress
but the fight gets harder. Combined with spawn-on-death for crabs, an evolved crab that
dies at level 5 spawns level-3 babies instead of level-2 — the threat cascades.

---

## 6. Sight & aggro system (shared)

All foes share the same sight/aggro state machine:

```
PATROL ──(player in sight)──▶ AGGRO ──(3 consecutive misses)──▶ PATROL
```

### Sight check

- Each foe type defines its sight shape, check interval, and improved variant (see §3–4).
- A sight check runs on a per-foe timer (1 s for Crab, 2 s for Seagull).
- "Miss" = player is not inside the sight shape.
- "Hit" = player is inside → immediately enter AGGRO.
- 3 **consecutive** misses while aggro'd → return to PATROL. A single hit resets the
  miss counter.

### Aggro behaviour

- Crab: move horizontally toward player's X. Stays on ground.
- Seagull: accelerate horizontally toward player's X. Maintains patrol height.

### De-aggro

After 3 consecutive misses, the foe resumes its Patrol behaviour from its current
position. The miss counter resets to 0 on re-aggro.

---

## 7. Loot & drops (hook only — content TBD)

Every foe has a virtual death hook that fires before `QueueFree()`:

```
OnDeath() → spawn loot (override per type) → QueueFree()
```

Reserved loot types for future implementation:

| Drop | Trigger | Effect |
|------|---------|--------|
| Heal orb | % chance on kill | Restore 15–30 HP |
| Ult charge orb | % chance on kill | +200–500 ult charge (Ult system TODO — TBD registry) |
| Wonder core(s) | Chance on kill / mission mechanics | The run currency (`ITEMS.gdd` §1.2, `NODES.gdd` §5.3) |
| Status cleanse | Rare drop | Clears OnFire/Frozen |

The architecture supports drops without knowing what they are — each foe type's
`OnDeath()` override instantiates whatever pickups it needs. Crab's override also
handles the Spawn skill before calling the base loot logic.

---

## 8. Boss fights

Landed in **v0.10.11** with the first boss, PumpKing. A boss is not a bigger foe. At the
**4-1 of every realm the player faces that realm's protagonist as a BOSS, and only beating
it recruits it** (`NODES.gdd` §4.5). Since **v0.11.0** every realm's 4-1 is a boss fight:
PumpKing stands in wherever a realm's own boss is not built yet (§8.1).

### 8.1 What a boss is

- **The protagonist's kit, on the other side.** A boss is the protagonist's own class,
  inherited — `class BossPumpKing : PumpKing` — fighting on the Foe side. Its head, shell and
  Soul Form are the ones the player will wear after recruiting it; nothing is re-implemented.
- **Not the protagonist.** A boss has its own row in `BossTable` (§8.2) and reads everything
  that differs from there: bulk, damage, poise, magazine, cooldowns, a held item. It is named
  BOSS everywhere — class `BossX`, scene `Scenes/Bosses/BossX.tscn`, the bar reads "BOSS X".
- **Sides.** Every fighter is on a `CombatSide`: Player or Foe. A Foe-side fighter is in the
  group the player's attacks iterate (`enemy` — not `foe`: it is not ambient population),
  sits on the Foes collision layer, owns no camera, never reads the keyboard, and its own
  attacks look for the player. Attack code asks one question, "who is hostile to my side?",
  so one kit works on either side unchanged (`Docs/Tech/T10` §4a).
- **Taking damage.** A boss is hit the way a foe is, not the way the player is: **no post-hit
  i-frames** (the player is never gated to one hit per window), the player's ult charge is
  credited, and the kit's own mitigation still applies (PumpKing's shell soaks first).
  **Poise** scales the knockback and the gain-no window it takes.
- **Giving damage.** The kit's numbers × the row's `DamageScale` × the day's ATK multiplier.
  The player keeps their post-hit i-frames against it.
- **Every realm's 4-1 fields a boss (v0.11.0).** A world whose own protagonist has no boss
  kit yet fields the **stand-in** — `BossTable.StandIn`, which is BOSS PumpKing: the owner's
  interim rule, "use PumpKing Boss as the BOSS for every realm for now". So today he holds
  all eight capitals, his own Kingdom of Horror among them (it joined the pool in v0.11.0).
  A world gets its own row as its boss is built (`BossTable.HomeBossOf`), and the stand-in
  then covers only what is left. Beating him anywhere recruits PumpKing — once; after that
  he simply falls. The v0.5.0 scaled-up crab remains for a node that names no boss — the LV6
  core — and as the fallback when a boss's scene fails to load.

### 8.2 The BOSS row (`Scripts/Data/BossTable.cs`)

| Field | Meaning | BOSS PumpKing |
|-------|---------|---------------|
| `BaseHp` | HP at foe level 1, × the day's HP multiplier (§2) | **600** (protagonist: 200; the crab stand-in: 560) |
| `DamageScale` | share of the kit's damage at foe level 1, × the day's ATK multiplier | **0.6** — a head that lands: 18 on contact + 30 from the blast |
| `KnockbackTaken` | poise: share of an incoming shove | **0.4** |
| `StunTaken` | poise: share of an incoming gain-no window | **0.25** |
| `CooldownScale` | multiplier on the kit's skill cooldowns | **1** |
| `Ammo` | the boss's own basic-attack magazine | 1 head, 1.5 s reload |
| `HeldItemDefId` | a wonder item it holds | none — reserved: nothing reads it yet |
| `DecisionSeconds` | its brain's reaction time (§8.4) | **0.15 s** |

**Every value is a first-pass placeholder.** The owner postponed boss design — "maybe
larger magazine, quicker CD, extra holding item, or something else" (TBD #2) — so each of
those knobs exists and sits where the fight is playable, not balanced. What is decided: a boss is
*not identical* to its protagonist, and the differences are a table row, not code.

The day axis applies to a boss as to any foe: HP and damage scale with the day's foe level
(§2). Speed does not — a boss is not a faster protagonist.

**What changes a boss's attributes — examined against this design (v0.11.0).** The run check
(`40-QA.md`) enters a real run's 4-1 in every world, on a different day band in each, and
reads the boss it finds:

| Cause in the design | A foe | BOSS PumpKing | Checked |
|---------------------|-------|---------------|---------|
| The day → foe level 1–6 (§2): **HP** ×1 / 1.2 / 1.5 / 2 / 3 / 3 | yes | **yes** — **600 / 720 / 900 / 1200 / 1800 / 1800** HP on days 1–10 / 11–20 / 21–30 / 31–35 / 36–40 / 41–45, and the boss bar reads the same | every band, in a run |
| The day → **ATK** ×1 / 1.2 / 1.5 / 1.5 / 1.5 / 2 | yes | **yes** — ×0.60 / 0.72 / 0.90 / 0.90 / 0.90 / 1.20 of the kit's damage | every band, in a run |
| The day → **SPD** ×1 / 1.2 / 1.2 / 1.2 / 1.5 / 1.5 | yes | **no** — he walks and flies at the protagonist's speeds on every day | open, TBD #31 |
| **Evolution** (§5): not eliminated within 25 s → +1 level, max HP re-derived, healed 30% | yes — the crab stand-in did | **no** | open, TBD #31 |
| Zone 6: the node fixes the level (LV5 → 7, the core → 8), whatever the day | yes | not his case (he is an LV4 boss); *since v0.12.0 zone 6's bosses are its variant's own rows (§8.7)* — on his kit: a gate's 600 × 4 = **2400** HP, the core's 900 × 5 = **4500** | yes |
| Skills unlocked by level (§2) | yes | no — his whole rule list on every day (§8.4) | — |
| The kit's own state | — | Pump Up: +20 shell per stack, **not** scaled by the day, and slower while pumped | — |
| The player's own PumpKing (HP, held item, Sharpen bonuses) | — | **nothing carries over** — only the player's body is hydrated from the run | HP exact, in a run |

HP and damage are the design's, to the point. The two **no** rows are a deliberate
difference from a foe and the owner's to overturn (TBD #31). A 25-second evolution would
hand every boss a level and a 30% heal before the player has climbed to it — +540 HP on
days 36–40, with a timer whose expiry is permadeath — and a faster boss needs legs that do
not overshoot a perch at twice the speed. Neither should arrive by accident.

### 8.3 BOSS PumpKing

PumpKing's weapon is his own head, bowled. **On level ground it does little; on a slope it
does not stop** (`PumpKing.gdd`, Fire Head): thrown level it carries **7.7 m** and goes off;
set rolling on a 1:2 slope it runs the whole slope at its **6 m/s** roll cap. So he holds
high ground and bowls downhill, and his arena is built to let him (§8.5).

His hands and legs are separate rule lists (§8.4), most urgent first:

| Channel | Rule | When | Does |
|---------|------|------|------|
| legs | **soul flight** | in Soul Form | flies to the perch he chose: up clear of the higher end, across, down onto it |
| legs | **hold perch** | otherwise | walks back to his perch, jumps when under it or blocked, faces the player |
| hands | **detonate** | his rolling head is within 0.8 blast radii of the player | sets it off (BA) |
| hands | **call back** | his head has been rolling *away* from the player for 0.9 s, 1.5 radii clear | sets it off, so the magazine reloads |
| hands | **soul away** | the player has been in his face — within 3.5 m, about level — for 1.2 s, and Soul Form is ready | Soul Form (Shift); leaves for the perch farthest from the player |
| hands | **bowl** | head on his neck and pumped enough for the range | aims and throws (BA) |
| hands | **pump up** | not yet pumped enough for the range | Pump Up (E) |

- **Pumping by range.** Before a throw he wants 3 stacks with the player beyond 14 m, 2
  beyond 8 m, 1 beyond 5 m, none closer: the farther the player, the longer he can afford to
  build shell, and the more he will need it when they arrive. Up close he throws at once.
- **Aim.** A straight (low-arc) throw if it reaches the player; otherwise the angle that
  carries farthest for the drop, which from his balcony lands the head on the top of the
  slope. He leads a moving player by 0.25 s. Always inside the kit's firing segments.
- **Changing ground.** Soul Form is his way out, exactly as for the player: 12 s cooldown,
  0.5 s windup, 6 s of flight at 5 m/s, no attacking while it lasts. A head still rolling
  when he leaves loses its blast (the kit's own rule): it deals only its contact damage if it
  touches someone, nothing if it goes off by itself — unless he is back in time to set it off.
- **What a head costs** (foe level 1, measured). A head he sets off beside the player deals
  its blast: **30**. A head that rolls into them deals its contact damage *and* its blast —
  18 + 30 = **48** (0.6 of the 80 measured at ×1) — because a hit that carries no knockback
  starts no i-frames, and the touch carries none. So "detonate", as tuned, gives up the 18
  against a player who stands still; it is what catches one who jumps or steps aside.
  Whether he should let a closing head arrive is an open tuning question (TBD #2).
- **A wasted head.** A head rolling away from a player who got behind him would go off by
  itself 3.2 s later; "call back" sets it off after 1.2 s, and he has it again 1.6 s on.
- **What the player can do about it.** Climb: the slope costs a fifth of a fighter's speed
  going up and nothing coming down. Jump the heads — he sets a head off once it is about
  3.3 m from the player, centre to centre, and a jump is higher than that for about 0.8 s.
  Reach him: while his head is out he has no shell, and until Soul Form is back he has
  nowhere to go.

### 8.4 The brain: rule-based

A boss's brain presses the same buttons the player does. It owns a **virtual pad**; the kit
reads it exactly as it reads the keyboard, so a brain can never do what the kit does not
allow (cooldowns, the magazine, firing segments, state gates all still apply).

- **Rules.** "When *this*, do *that*." Each belongs to a **channel** — legs (`move`) or hands
  (`act`) — and one rule holds each channel at a time, so a boss walks and throws at once but
  never throws twice. Within a channel the rules are a priority list: the first one off
  cooldown whose condition holds wins.
- **Two kinds.** A *one-shot* taps a button once each time it wins, then waits out its
  cooldown. A *continuous* rule holds buttons for as long as it keeps winning. A rule may
  also *commit* for a time, during which nothing in its channel interrupts it.
- **Reaction time.** The brain decides every `DecisionSeconds` (0.15 s), not every frame,
  and senses the fight only then.
- **Readable.** With debug mode on, the mission line shows what each channel is doing
  (`[move:hold perch · act:bowl]`).
- **Rule-based is the first driver, not the only one.** Anything that can fill a virtual pad
  can drive a boss — a behaviour tree, a recorded input track, a trained policy — with no
  change to the kit, the mission or the map.

### 8.5 Boss arenas

A boss map takes its boss's side.

- **Goal `boss`.** A map tagged with it (`MapCreation.gdd` §9) is dealt to its world's LV4
  Boss mission ahead of the world's ordinary maps, and to nothing else. A world without one
  fights its boss on an ordinary map, as before.
- **Perches.** The map's Level Goal markers are the boss's perches: the places its kit works
  best from. The fight opens with the boss on the perch **farthest from the player**. Two
  perches let a boss change ground; one is enough to fight.
- **Every realm has one (v0.11.0).** Seven are plain test arenas in the realm's own kit
  (`Tools/realm_maps/build_band_maps.py`): a floor, a tower 12 m high on the east side
  climbed by two 6 m steps — the first perch, as far from the spawn as the map goes — and a
  ledge 18 m west of it, level with the first step — the second. They are valid, not
  flattering: only the Dark Wood has a slope block (TBD #30), so nowhere else does his head
  have a hill to run. Undersea (Loong's Nest) the whole arena is water. The run check stages
  him on each and he gets a head away at a standing player on all eight.
- **A boss in a soft volume (v0.11.1).** A fighter swims in every soft volume
  (`Gameplay.gdd` §A.2) and a boss is a fighter, so he can: on his own pad BOSS PumpKing
  swims 1.4 m/s up and 7.4 m/s down in the sea, and the player's Up and Down do nothing to
  him (the boss check measures both). **No rule of his uses it.** "Hold perch" walks and
  jumps; in water he sinks to the bed at 3 m/s as he did before, jumps in 2.9 m strokes and
  leaves in Soul Form — and in foliage or cloud he now floats down at a tenth of his top
  speed (0.8 m/s, from the model; not measured on him) where he used to fall through. Three
  boss arenas hold soft volumes: the undersea one (all water), the beach's (two clouds) and
  his castle (one bramble); the boss check and the run check pass on them, and nothing in
  them sends him through a volume on purpose. Teaching his legs to swim is undesigned, and
  has a catch: pumped to three he is slowed to 4.8 m/s, and at that speed holding Up in the
  sea still sinks (0.4 m/s, from the model — `MapCreation.gdd` §2.2a). TBD #34.
- **PumpKing's Castle** (`Maps/horror_pumpkings_castle.json`, `RealmStyleBible.md` §7A). From
  the left: a strip of wood where the player arrives; one unbroken 1:2 slope, 36 m along and
  18 m up; the forecourt, the gate (shut — nobody goes in) and the balcony he comes out onto,
  6 m above the forecourt and a clean jump from it; the keep behind seals the east side. His
  perches are the balcony's outer end and a limb over the upper slope, 19 m apart — one Soul
  Form flight. From either, a head thrown for distance lands on the slope and runs it:
  measured in the fight, his first head travels **51 m** from the balcony and reaches a player
  standing at the foot.

### 8.6 The fight

- **Timer** as before: 240 s at LV4, expiry is fatal (`NODES.gdd` §4.5).
- **The boss owns the field.** The ambient spawner is off; two foes at foe level −1 arrive
  once, when the boss falls below half health.
- **The bar** names the boss and shows its HP, with the shell beside it while he has one.
- **Beating it** resolves the mission, grants the node's reward and **recruits the realm's
  own protagonist** — once: a protagonist already on the roster is not added again. The body
  fades and frees itself; there are no lives on the Foe side.
- **The recruit belongs to the realm, not the kit (v0.12.8, `NODES.gdd` §3.1, §4.5).** A run
  starts with its home protagonist alone (the prototype roster that owned all five ended).
  The handshake names the recruit (`AdventureContext.RecruitId`, the realm's home
  protagonist) beside the boss, and the mission pays it — so BOSS PumpKing standing in at
  Starland's 4-1 recruits Cleopastar, and nobody where no protagonist lives yet. A realm's own
  boss row must name the realm's home protagonist (the recruit check holds the two tables
  together).

### 8.7 Zone 6's bosses (v0.12.0, provisional — `Docs/Zone6.gdd` is their home)

Zone 6 is a rotating stage (`MapGDD.md` §7): each variant fields **five mini-bosses** on its
LV5 nodes and **one final boss** on the core. They are `BossTable` rows like any boss, with
`Role` = `Zone6Mini` / `Zone6Final`, world `XX`, the variant they belong to, and **no
protagonist**: they recruit nobody and pay no item (`BossDef.Recruits` is false).

| Variant | Mini-bosses (XX-5-1 … 5) | Final boss (XX-6-1) |
|---------|--------------------------|---------------------|
| The Void Frontier | the Ferryman · the Weigher of Hearts · Meng Po · Xolotl · Stingy Jack | **the Unwritten** — signature **Styx Tide** |
| Avīci | Preta (贪) · Asura of Wrath (嗔) · the Boar of Delusion (痴) · the Fallen Deva (慢) · Ox-Head and Horse-Face (疑) | **Māra Pāpīyas** — on his own kit since v0.12.3, his Wheel of Becoming turning behind him; each gate won seals a realm the wheel then skips (`Mara.gdd`) |

- **Stand-in.** Until its own kit is built, every one fields BOSS PumpKing's kit under its own
  row: its name on the bar, its HP (600 for a mini-boss, 900 for a final boss, at foe level 1,
  × the node's level — 7 or 8), damage share 0.6, PumpKing's poise and magazine. The concept
  line in each row is what its kit is to be (`Zone6.gdd` §4–§5).
- **Level.** The node fixes it (§2): 7 at a gate, 8 at the core — a gate's mini-boss has
  600 × 4 = 2400 HP, the core's 900 × 5 = 4500.
- **Losing to a mini-boss is survived** (`NODES.gdd` §2.2): the run goes on, the variant's
  penalty is paid. Its timer (240 s) is a defeat, not a death. The final boss keeps both.
- **A signature** is a field attack the fight runs whatever kit stands in (`BossDef.Signature`):
  the Unwritten's **Styx Tide** — a 5.5 m wave across the whole floor every 9 s; two in a row
  below half HP, the second from the other side below a quarter (`Zone6.gdd` §4.2).
- **Māra's seals** are realms his wheel skips (v0.12.3, `Mara.gdd` §4): he keeps his row's HP
  and damage. The v0.12.0 stand-in rule (−12 % HP and −10 damage per seal) is retired.
- **Debug.** The debug page (key 4) lists realm bosses one button each, and zone 6's twelve in
  one picker with a Fight button; a zone-6 fight is staged at its node's level, on the map its
  node would be dealt, with the rules a run would carry.
- **Own kits (v0.12.1): the Ferryman and the Asura of Wrath.** A zone-6 kit is a Foe-side
  fighter that is nobody's protagonist (`Scripts/Bosses/Zone6/Zone6BossFighter.cs`): its row's
  scene (`Scenes/Bosses/Ferryman.tscn`, `AsuraWrath.tscn`) and magazine, a brain of its own
  rules on a virtual pad, one static sprite (drawn facing left; the scene's negative x scale
  mirrors it so the body's facing flips it), the body's own hit flash. Cue owners `ferryman`,
  `asura_wrath`. Skills and measured numbers: `Zone6.gdd` §4.1, §5.1. The other ten still
  field the stand-in.

---

## 9. Architecture overview

```
Scripts/Foes/
├── BaseFoe.cs         // shared: HP, scaling, sight, aggro FSM, evolution, status effects,
│                      //   defense calc, external velocity, hazard interaction, loot hook,
│                      //   ult-charge crediting, damage popups
├── CrabFoe.cs         // ground physics, rectangle sight, Jump, Spawn-on-death
├── SeagullFoe.cs      // no-gravity flight physics, cone sight, Poop, Dash
└── FoeStats.cs        // per-level multiplier table, evolution data, helper: StatsForLevel()
```

Bosses are not in this folder: a boss is a protagonist's class on the Foe side (§8).
`Scripts/Bosses/` holds the brain (`BossBrain.cs`: rules, scheduler, senses), what every boss
shares (`BossFighter.cs`) and one class per boss; `Scripts/Data/BossTable.cs` holds their rows.

`BaseFoe` inherits `CharacterBody2D`. It provides the physics loop, status effect
handling (fire/frozen/burn), damage reception (defense multiplier, knockback, stun,
hitflash, camera shake), and the sight/aggro FSM. Subclasses override:

- `InitFoe()` — set base stats, sight shape, patrol behaviour
- `UpdatePatrol(dt)` / `UpdateAggro(dt)` — movement per state
- `Skill1()` / `Skill2()` — active skills (guarded by level gating in base)
- `OnDeath()` — spawn babies, drop loot, then `QueueFree()`
- `DrawSight()` — debug-draw the current sight shape

The existing `Scripts/Enemy.cs` is replaced by this hierarchy. `Enemy.tscn` is either
replaced or becomes a generic foe scene with the script swapped per type.

### Level-gating skills

`BaseFoe` checks `CurrentLevel` before allowing skill use. Skills are registered with
a `minLevel`; the base `_PhysicsProcess` only calls `Skill1()` / `Skill2()` if the foe's
current level meets the threshold.

### FoeStats lookup

```csharp
public static class FoeStats
{
    public struct LevelStats { public int Level; public float HpMult, AtkMult, SpdMult; }
    public static LevelStats ForLevel(int level);          // 1–8
    public static bool HasSkill1(int level);               // level >= 4
    public static bool HasSkill2(int level);               // level >= 6
    public static bool HasImprovedSight(int level);        // level >= 3
    public static bool HasAggro(int level);                // level >= 2
}
```

---

## 10. Foe ↔ Map integration

When the player enters a combat node on the map, the arena loads with:

1. **Node level** (1–6) → decides the fight's objective (wave count, foe count, win
   condition, boss presence). *This is the fight-designer's knob.*
2. **Current day** (1–45) → decides every spawned foe's **foe level** via the table
   in §2. *This is the difficulty-curve knob.*

A level-1 combat node on day 35 spawns level-5 foes with a simple objective. A level-4
boss node on day 12 spawns level-2 foes with a complex objective. The player balances
pushing deeper nodes against letting days accumulate.

**Authored map spawn contract (v0.7.0):** a combat map carries a per-hardship crab/seagull
weight table (level 0 = its default) and optional enemy-spawn row limits. The periodic spawner
ticks every **4 s**, filters crab/seagull types that lack an eligible map marker, then makes the
weighted choice. The selected marker is the foe's nest/spawn origin; map-defined crab limits use
`cellY <= crabMax`, while seagull limits use `cellY >= seagullMin`. Seashore uses 50/50 and
`crabMax=4`, `seagullMin=5`.

---

## 11. Arena foe cap

- **Hard cap:** 6 foes on screen (raised from the current 4 to accommodate spawn-on-death).
- **Spawn-on-death ignores the cap** — if a crab dies and the arena is at cap, the 2
  baby crabs still spawn (otherwise the skill is trivially negated). The cap only gates
  the GameManager's periodic spawn timer.
- **Evolution is unaffected by cap** — it modifies an existing foe, not a new spawn.

---

## 12. Decisions log

- **HP scaling uses base-relative multipliers, not cumulative.** A level-8 foe's HP is
  exactly `baseHP × 5`. This keeps the curve flat enough that combat times stay reasonable.
- **Crab skills swapped** (Spawn = skill 1 / on-death, Jump = skill 2 / active) so early
  game doesn't introduce spawn cascades before the player has tools.
- **Seagull dash pierces** rather than stopping on contact — it's a swoop, not a tackle.
- **Spawned crabs cannot spawn** — infinite chains are funny exactly once, then they break
  the game.
- **Sight shapes:** Crab = ground-anchored rectangle (vertical escape), Seagull = movement
  cone (positional outplay). Different evasion tactics per foe type.
- **Evolution heals 30%** (not 100%) so the player's progress isn't erased, only dampened.
- **Poop lingers 3 s** (up from the initial 0.5 s) — long enough to zone but short enough
  that the arena doesn't fill permanently.
- **(v0.3.7) Zone-6 foe levels are per-node** (LV5 → 7, LV6 core → 8); the day table
  ends at the singularity.
- **(v0.3.7) Spawn's level-1–3 bullets deleted** — the level-4+ gate is authoritative,
  so spawns are always level-(N−2) ≥ 2; evolution is the only path for a lower crab to
  gain the skill.
- **(v0.3.7) Seagull dash speed fixed at 30 m/s** (15 m in 0.5 s, after the 1 s red
  telegraph). Note the 100 m patrol turnaround exceeds the ~62 m standard arena — a
  patrolling gull effectively wall-turns.
- **(v0.3.7) Evolution HP formula canonized:** ratio-preserved, then +30% of new max,
  capped at new max (see §5).
- **(v0.3.7) WonderPage → wonder core.** The loot hook drops the run currency; the old
  page-based win condition was prototype-0 history.
- **(v0.5.0) Skill dispatch implemented as per-frame `UpdateSkills(dt, player)` with
  `Skill1Ready`/`Skill2Ready` gates**, not the §9 parameterless `Skill1()/Skill2()`
  calls — trigger conditions need per-skill state (telegraphs, cooldowns), so the base
  loop hands control to the subclass each frame instead of invoking blindly. Level
  gating (`FoeStats.HasSkill1/2`) is unchanged and still authoritative inside the
  Ready gates. Functionally equivalent to §9; recorded so the doc matches the code.
- **(v0.5.0) Seagull top flight speed = 160 px/s (5 m/s)** — the GDD was silent;
  tuning placeholder, revisit on a feel pass.
- **(v0.5.0) Wave/arena composition prototype defaults** (per-world composition tables
  still TBD): every ambient/wave slot rolls 60% crab / 40% seagull; ambient cap 6 with
  a 3 s trickle (the cap gates only the trickle, per §11); Slaughter wave size =
  3 + node level, final wave at foe level +1 capped at 8.
- **(v0.5.0) Placeholder boss = scaled-up Crab** (HP ×8, ~×2 footprint, beefier
  contact; one add-wave of 2 foes at foe level −1 when the boss hits 50% HP) until the
  §8 boss kits are designed. Outer BOSS wins grant Yukai's Rope; the starting-realm LV4
  also grants TwistedReality. Recruitable boss protagonists remain TBD.
- **(v0.10.11) A boss is its protagonist's class on the other side, not a foe class.** The
  alternative — a `BaseFoe` that re-implements the kit — would make every boss two kits to
  keep in step, which is exactly the scope bomb `NODES.gdd` flagged. Inheriting makes the
  boss fight with the moves the player will recruit, for free. The stand-in crab stays for
  worlds without a kit.
- **(v0.10.11) One interface for "something an attack can land on".** Every kit used to look
  for `BaseFoe` in the `enemy` group, so nothing a protagonist did could hit another
  protagonist's class. A hurtbox that pretended to be a foe was considered and rejected: two
  bodies for one fighter, forwarding a dozen calls, entering every hazard twice. Instead
  foes and fighters are both `ICombatTarget`, and attacks ask who is hostile to their side.
- **(v0.10.11) The brain presses buttons.** A rule cannot do what the kit forbids, a boss
  cannot drift from its protagonist, and the kind of AI is replaceable behind one seam.
  Rule-based first, as the owner asked.
- **(v0.10.11) No i-frames on a boss; poise instead.** The 0.8 s window exists to protect a
  player. On a boss it would cap every protagonist at one hit per window, and full hitstun
  would let a fast combo hold him still. Poise is two numbers in his row.
- **(v0.10.11) A boss scales with the day as foes do** — HP and damage, not speed — so the
  day axis keeps its meaning in the one room where it matters most (§1).
- **(v0.10.11) A boss arena is a map goal, and its perches are the goal markers the map
  format already had.** No new marker kind, no new editor tool; a map author already knows
  how to place them. The boss opens on the one farthest from the player.
- **(v0.10.11) PumpKing's head now really rolls**, which is what his arena is built on. Until
  now a head resting on a floor lost 15% of its speed every physics tick and stopped within
  0.7 m; it could not have run a slope. See `PumpKing.gdd`.
- **(v0.10.11) A red mark hangs over the boss.** Placeholder: a black-coated PumpKing with
  his head off is nearly invisible against his castle's black stone, and a fighter must read
  first. To be replaced by his boss art.
- **(v0.11.0) PumpKing stands in as every realm's boss** — the owner's interim rule, so that
  every 4-1 is a real boss fight to test against. It is one fallback in `BossTable.ForWorld`
  (the world's own boss, else the stand-in), not eight rows: a world that gets its own boss
  takes itself out of it by adding a row. He recruits once and then just falls, so meeting
  him five times in a run says "joins you" at most once.
- **(v0.11.0) A boss's attributes were examined against the foe design** (§8.2's table): HP
  and damage follow the day exactly, checked in a real run on every day band; speed and the
  25-second evolution do not apply to a boss, on purpose, and are the owner's to change
  (TBD #31). What looked like a third question — whether the player's own PumpKing leaks into
  the boss — is not one: only the player's body is hydrated from the run.
